{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"agent_message","text":"## Overall verdict: PARTIAL\n\nThe note correctly identifies that the cited tests do **not** satisfy §8.10.11. Its claim that the real-source tests “bypass the convention” is too broad, though: the rewritten TS caller invokes through `yang.call/invoke-with`, and the rewritten PHP caller invokes a `:yang.call/entry` using the convention-shaped invocation record. The disqualifying issue is that the harness rewrites the caller’s lowered AST and injects a donor function.\n\n### 1. Cross-language tests rewrite the caller AST — CONFIRMED\n\nIn [`cross_lang_test.clj`](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yang/cross_lang_test.clj:110), `ts-into-php` postwalks the lowered PHP source and replaces every `php/resolve-fn` application with `%tsfn` (lines 116–121). In [`php-into-ts`](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yang/cross_lang_test.clj:125), the postwalk replaces the matching `js/global-get \"phpfn\"` with `%phpfn` (lines 130–136). Both bind that variable to `fn-ast` produced from a separately lowered donor program (lines 114–115, 129–130, 122, 137).\n\nSo the note is right that these tests do not meet the no-rewriting gate. The calls still use the entry protocol: TypeScript `js/call` routes through `yang.call/invoke-with` ([`prelude.cljc`](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/typescript/antlr/prelude.cljc:1595)), while PHP `php/call` constructs an invocation record and calls the callee’s `:yang.call/entry` ([`prelude.cljc`](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc:2080)). That makes “bypass the convention” an imprecise description of these tests; the AST substitutions are the clear conformance defect.\n\nThe spec says: “Execute real lowered source bodies” and “must not rewrite call nodes, extract foreign `:code`, or insert test-local language-pair wrappers” ([`yang.antlr.md`](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.antlr.md:4012)).\n\n### 2. `call_test` uses synthetic entries — CONFIRMED\n\n[`cross-frontend-callable-identity-fixture-test`](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yang/core/call_test.cljc:202) assembles its PHP, TS, and Python callable values in the test itself. For example, the PHP entry is an inline `fn` at lines 206–211, and the TypeScript relay’s entry is another inline `fn` at lines 214–221. The second fixture likewise supplies inline entries at lines 234–260.\n\nThese fixtures exercise `yang.call/invoke` and `invoke-with`, but they do not obtain the callables by lowering PHP or TS source. The claim that they do not establish real lowerer admission, binding, or receiver behavior is supported.\n\n### 3. No qualifying real-source × convention test for TS/PHP — PARTIAL\n\nI found no other TS/PHP test combining lowered foreign-call source with the convention in a way that meets §8.10.11. The broader search found the cross-language tests above, synthetic `:yang.call/entry` fixtures in [`prelude_parity_test.cljc`](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yang/typescript/antlr/prelude_parity_test.cljc:744), and convention/runtime tests—not another qualifying source-call test. The spec requires direct calls between implemented frontends in both directions and higher-order calls through received functions ([`yang.antlr.md`](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.antlr.md:4022)).\n\nHowever, the note’s wording that the real-source tests “bypass the convention” does not fully hold: the rewritten TS call reaches `yang.call/invoke-with`, and the rewritten PHP call reaches a `:yang.call/entry`. The supported conclusion is narrower: those tests fail the acceptance gate because of the AST rewrites and donor injection, while the other convention fixtures use synthetic cells. Thus the tree lacks **qualifying §8.10.11 evidence** for TS/PHP, even though the two existing test layers do exercise parts of the source and convention mechanisms.\n\nRead-only review; no files were changed and no tests were run."}}
